The "both accepted and pending" test only creates the pending membership if some other team happens to exist in the DB, and its assertion changes to match:

::code{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.integration-test.ts" lines="127-158"}

If `team2` is null, the test checks that a user with only an accepted membership returns `false`. That's the same as test 2, and the mixed case is never exercised. A test whose expected result depends on environment data gives false confidence. Create a second team in `beforeAll` (and delete it in `afterAll`) so the scenario is deterministic, then assert `toBe(true)` unconditionally.

Each test creates a `User` and deletes it at the end of the test body, after `expect`:

::code{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.integration-test.ts" lines="68-74"}

When an assertion fails (as it will with the current `accepted: true` bug), the user deletion never runs and the row stays in the shared test DB. Memberships are cleaned up in `afterEach`, but users aren't. Track created user IDs the same way as `createdMembershipIds` and delete them in `afterEach`, or wrap each test body in `try/finally`.

The only change to the existing line switched it to single quotes, while every other string in the file (and the repo) uses double quotes:

::diff{file="apps/web/app/(use-page-wrapper)/onboarding/getting-started/page.tsx" lines="35"}

